confidence: 62 | The data exists once the queue rule is in; whether the rail helps or distracts is unproven.
sources: docs/product-ux-audit/07-debate-and-arbitration.md (ruling 3: queue rail); docs/product-ux-audit/08-design-system-blueprint.md (section 8); docs/product-ux-audit/10-prioritized-implementation-backlog.md (audit backlog UX-14 split, audit backlog UX-10 prototype); docs/product-ux-audit/reviews/reviewer-a-rebuttal.md (section 3, wrong patient); docs/product-ux-audit/reviews/reviewer-b-rebuttal.md (section 2)
desc:
Programme: MDV-P1-E12 · UX programme: safe, cohesive clinic workspace (from the Nov 2026 product UX audit). Track D (design rules and UI component setup), wave D2. No audit backlog id. Created from the audit's rulings and blueprint, not from a backlog line (see Findings).

Persona and route: /consult/:id · doctor · 1280 px and wider as a rail; 1024 to 1279 as a button in the strip.
Findings and reviewers: F-07, F-04 (to finish) · reviewers A-08 · arbitration ruling 3. Named as a separate ticket by audit backlog UX-14; no UX number of its own.
Rationale: The doctor cannot see who is next, or that a nurse escalated someone, without leaving the note. The first concept showed other patients' names and reasons beside the open note, in view of the patient in the room: the arbitration rejected that.
Proposed UX behaviour: At 1280 px and wider a 200 px rail beside the note lists the doctor's queue in the clinic's queue order: token and age band only, escalations first with the word Escalated (no reason text), then To finish (notes to sign, prescriptions to authorise) as counts. Never a name, a reason or a diagnosis. From 1024 to 1279 px the rail is one button in the strip: Next A-07 · 3 waiting. Choosing another patient keeps today's You have another consultation open challenge. The rail is styled unlike the section anchors, so moving to another chart is a deliberate, distinct act. It can be hidden by the doctor.
Prototype and blueprint reference: docs/product-ux-audit/concepts/medvya-workspace-concept.html, doctor view, audit note 3 (as changed after the debate); docs/product-ux-audit/concepts/screenshots/doctor-1440.png and doctor-1024.png.
Components and tokens affected: SplitLayout region; Chip. No new component.
Front end and back end split: Front end only; reads the ordered queue that the queue-rule ticket exposes.
Prerequisites and blocked by: Blocked by the consultation layout ticket and the queue-rule ticket.
Clinical sign-off: Ask the pilot doctor in the session (doctor scenario step 9) whether the rail helps; not a merge gate.
Effort and regression risk: M · Medium: a list of other patients beside an open note is a wrong-patient risk if it ever shows names; the test below guards that.
Deliberately NOT in this ticket: (1) Names, reasons or complaints in the rail. (2) Switching patient without the open-consultation challenge.
ac:
- Given a queue of five patients, when the rail renders, then each row shows a token and an age band and the page holds no other patient's name, complaint or escalation reason
- Given the rail's test, when a name is added to a row, then the test fails (mutation check)
- Given an escalated visit, when the rail renders, then it is the first row and carries the word Escalated
- Given the clinic's queue rule, when the rail and My day are compared, then the order is the same
- Given an open consultation, when another token is chosen, then the another consultation is open challenge appears and nothing changes until the doctor answers
- Given 1024 px, when the strip renders, then the rail is the button Next with the token and the waiting count; and axe finds no violation in English or Arabic
tests:
- New steps in tests/e2e/consult-studio.spec.ts: rail content, the challenge, the tablet button.
- Real stack: after the handoff walkthrough, the escalated visit is row 1 of the rail.
notes:
- Regression risk: medium.
- May be dropped after the pilot doctor's session without loss to any other ticket.

=== MDV-P1-100
title: Front desk: undo the last move, Left without being seen, cancel a checked-in visit; the desk cannot close a visit under the doctor
type: Story
epic: MDV-P1-E12
prd: P1-08
screens: P-01, P-05
priority: MUST
roles: Receptionist, Nurse, Doctor
labels: front-desk, board, queue, safety, audit, epic: ux programme, track: safety, wave: S2
estimate: 4
weeks: 5-6
confidence: 65 | A visit state-machine change with new end states; who marks in the room and done is an open product decision.
sources: docs/product-ux-audit/10-prioritized-implementation-backlog.md (audit backlog UX-07); docs/product-ux-audit/04-evidence-backed-findings.md (F-05); docs/product-ux-audit/reviews/reviewer-a-clinical-workflow.md (A-06); docs/product-ux-audit/07-debate-and-arbitration.md (ruling 4); docs/product-ux-audit/05-accessibility-responsive-and-reliability.md (SC 3.3.4); docs/03-architecture (state machines)
desc:
Programme: MDV-P1-E12 · UX programme: safe, cohesive clinic workspace (from the Nov 2026 product UX audit). Track S (safety, trust and handoff behaviour), wave S2. This is audit backlog UX-07 of the Nov 2026 product UX audit (an audit id, not an MDV key).

Persona and route: /front-desk cards; the queue display; /today · receptionist, nurse, doctor.
Findings and reviewers: F-05 · reviewer A-06.
Rationale: Call in and Consult done are single taps with no confirmation and no undo. The desk closed a visit while the doctor's consultation stayed open. A walk-in who leaves must be pushed through to Done, which records a visit that never happened; the display calls the wrong token and the counts are wrong.
Proposed UX behaviour: After any move a status line offers Undo for 30 seconds, or until the next move; the server refuses an undo after the window. A card menu offers Left without being seen and Cancel visit, each with a reason; both remove the token from the queue display and are counted apart from Done. When a consultation is open for the visit, the desk's Consult done is replaced by With the doctor (no action), and In consult and Done follow the doctor's Start and End; the desk buttons stay for clinics whose doctors do not use the consultation screen. Two people changing one visit: the second is told the state changed, not silently overwritten. Every reversal and new end state is audited.
Prototype and blueprint reference: docs/product-ux-audit/concepts/medvya-workspace-concept.html, receptionist view, Undo last move (audit note 10); screenshot docs/product-ux-audit/concepts/screenshots/desk-1440-checked-in.png.
Components and tokens affected: Board card menu and status line; the existing Dialog for reasons. No new component.
Front end and back end split: Back end: reverse transitions with rules and audit, two new end states, the open-consultation guard, a version check on visit state. Front end: undo line, card menu, With the doctor. ERD and state-machine docs regenerated if a column or state is added.
Prerequisites and blocked by: None in code. Product decision: who marks in the room and done.
Clinical sign-off: Not clinical sign-off, but two decisions block merge: questions 11 and 12 of the validation plan (pilot doctor and receptionist), recorded on the issue. Safe acceptance criterion 4 applies: the receptionist recovers from a wrong Call in without help.
Effort and regression risk: L · Medium: the visit state machine is used by the board, the display, Today and the metrics.
Deliberately NOT in this ticket: (1) Reopening a completed and signed visit. (2) A no-show change (exists). (3) Drag and drop. (4) The board's chrome (track D).
ac:
- Given a card moved by Call in, when Undo is pressed within 30 seconds, then the card is back in Ready for doctor with the same token
- Given a move older than the window or followed by another move, when an undo is sent, then the server refuses it
- Given a checked-in walk-in, when the desk marks Left without being seen with a reason, then the token leaves the queue display and the visit is not counted as done
- Given an open consultation for the visit, when the desk views the card, then it reads With the doctor with no action, and a direct API call to close it is refused
- Given an undo or a new end state, when the queue display and Today are watched, then both follow within one live update
- Given any undo, cancel or left-without-being-seen, when the audit log is read, then it holds who, when, the reason and the previous state
- Given the integration tests, when the window rule or the open-consultation guard is removed, then a test fails
- Given the walk-in path, when it is measured, then it is still at most 6 clicks new and 3 returning; and the card menu works by keyboard with no axe violation in English or Arabic
tests:
- Integration (real PostgreSQL): transitions, window, guard, tenant isolation, audit rows.
- Playwright J3 new steps (tests/e2e/fullstack/j3.spec.ts): Call in → Undo → card back in Ready for doctor; mark left → card gone from the display list; desk cannot close a visit with an open consultation.
- tests/e2e/queue-tv.spec.ts and front-desk.spec.ts updated.
notes:
- Regression risk: medium.

=== MDV-P1-101
title: Patient picker rows show date of birth and last visit, and mark possible duplicates
type: Story
epic: MDV-P1-E12
prd: P1-05, P2-01
screens: P-04, D-02, P-03
priority: MUST
roles: Receptionist, Doctor
labels: patients, search, safety, privacy, dedup, epic: ux programme, track: safety, wave: S2
estimate: 2
weeks: 5
confidence: 75 | Last visit and the merge flag must be added to search results; the date of birth at a shared desk is a privacy decision.
sources: docs/product-ux-audit/10-prioritized-implementation-backlog.md (audit backlog UX-08); docs/product-ux-audit/04-evidence-backed-findings.md (F-08); docs/product-ux-audit/reviews/reviewer-a-clinical-workflow.md (A-05); docs/product-ux-audit/reviews/reviewer-a-rebuttal.md (sections 2 and 4)
desc:
Programme: MDV-P1-E12 · UX programme: safe, cohesive clinic workspace (from the Nov 2026 product UX audit). Track S (safety, trust and handoff behaviour), wave S2. This is audit backlog UX-08 of the Nov 2026 product UX audit (an audit id, not an MDV key).

Persona and route: Walk-in, booking and waitlist pickers; header search · receptionist, doctor.
Findings and reviewers: F-08 · reviewer A-05.
Rationale: Two Deepak Nair, 47 y, Male rows differed only in the last four MRN digits; a hurried person picks the first, which was the duplicate. Visits, vitals and allergies then split across two charts.
Proposed UX behaviour: Every result row, on two lines: name, age and sex, date of birth, masked MRN, masked phone, last visit date. A record with an open merge request shows the words Possible duplicate; the older record sorts first and is marked Main record. Rows are comfortable height (never compact) and the whole row is the target. The doctor's header search shows the same fields in place of initials only, within what the role may already search. Show full numbers stays a logged reveal.
Prototype and blueprint reference: docs/product-ux-audit/concepts/medvya-workspace-concept.html, receptionist view: type 90000 (audit note 8); screenshots docs/product-ux-audit/concepts/screenshots/desk-1440-phone-lookup.png and desk-1440-confirm-dob.png.
Components and tokens affected: The shared picker row; Tag for Possible duplicate and Main record. No new component.
Front end and back end split: Front end; back end adds last visit and the merge flag to the search results the role may already see.
Prerequisites and blocked by: None in code. Privacy decision: date of birth in a list the desk already may search.
Clinical sign-off: Decision required before merge: question 15 of the validation plan (owner and receptionist), recorded on the issue.
Effort and regression risk: M · Low. Search authorisation and masking must not widen.
Deliberately NOT in this ticket: (1) One find-or-check-in box (a new flow; not before the pilot). (2) Unmasking MRN or phone. (3) Merge itself.
ac:
- Given two patients with the same name, age and sex, when the picker lists them, then the rows differ in at least two shown fields (date of birth, last visit or the duplicate mark)
- Given a record with an open merge request, when it is listed, then it reads Possible duplicate and the older record is first and reads Main record
- Given a search, when the address bar, logs and telemetry are read, then they hold no name, phone digits or date of birth
- Given Show full numbers, when it is used, then an audit row is written as today
- Given a role that may not search clinical data, when the search endpoint is called, then the answer holds only the desk fields, and the test fails if last visit exposes a diagnosis or note text
- Given a touch device, when rows are measured, then each is at least 48 px tall and the whole row is the target; axe finds no violation in English or Arabic
tests:
- Integration: search result shape per role, tenant isolation.
- Playwright (tests/e2e/patients.spec.ts, global-search.spec.ts; J3 step 2): search 0102 → two rows with different dates of birth or a duplicate mark.
- Walk-in click count unchanged.
notes:
- Regression risk: low.

=== MDV-P1-102
title: The doctor's follow-up reaches the front desk: Follow-up due on the Done card and the desk record, with Book
type: Story
epic: MDV-P1-E12
prd: P1-06, P2-04
screens: P-01, P-04
priority: MUST
roles: Receptionist, Doctor
labels: front-desk, booking, encounters, care, epic: ux programme, track: safety, wave: S2
estimate: 2
weeks: 6
confidence: 78 | The follow-up is stored and printed; it has to be exposed to desk roles as a date only.
sources: docs/product-ux-audit/10-prioritized-implementation-backlog.md (audit backlog UX-09); docs/product-ux-audit/04-evidence-backed-findings.md (F-06); docs/product-ux-audit/reviews/reviewer-a-clinical-workflow.md (A-07); docs/05-delivery/tickets/P2-doctor-workspace/MDV-P2-043.md
desc:
Programme: MDV-P1-E12 · UX programme: safe, cohesive clinic workspace (from the Nov 2026 product UX audit). Track S (safety, trust and handoff behaviour), wave S2. This is audit backlog UX-09 of the Nov 2026 product UX audit (an audit id, not an MDV key).

Persona and route: Board Done card, desk patient record, schedule · receptionist.
Findings and reviewers: F-06 · reviewer A-07. A verified defect against an acceptance line of MDV-P2-043.
Rationale: The doctor's screen says The front desk will book it from Tuesday, 17 November 2026. The desk sees nothing. Chronic-care patients are not rebooked unless they ask.
Proposed UX behaviour: The Done card and the desk patient record show Follow-up due from 17 Nov · Book. Book opens the booking panel on that date with the patient and doctor chosen. The line clears when the follow-up is booked, or when the desk dismisses it with a reason. The desk sees the date and the doctor only, never the note or the reason for the follow-up.
Prototype and blueprint reference: docs/product-ux-audit/concepts/medvya-workspace-concept.html, receptionist view, desk jobs beside the queue (audit note 10).
Components and tokens affected: Board card, desk record, booking panel. No new component.
Front end and back end split: Front end; back end exposes the follow-up date to desk roles through an application contract of the encounters module (no cross-module table access), with no clinical text.
Prerequisites and blocked by: None.
Clinical sign-off: Not required. The receptionist confirms in the session (receptionist scenario step 5).
Effort and regression risk: M · Low.
Deliberately NOT in this ticket: (1) Automatic booking. (2) Reminders to the patient. (3) Care-plan tasks (P4).
ac:
- Given the doctor set a follow-up of 2 weeks and ended the visit, when the receptionist opens the board and the patient's desk record, then both show Follow-up due from the date with Book
- Given the line, when Book is pressed, then the booking panel opens on that date with the patient and doctor chosen
- Given the follow-up is booked or dismissed with a reason, when the board refreshes, then the line is gone and a dismissal is audited
- Given a desk role, when the follow-up is returned by the API, then it holds the date and doctor and no note text, and the test fails if clinical text is added
- Given that ticket's line The follow-up choice appears on the front desk's list for that patient, when the journey runs, then it passes on the real stack
- Given English and Arabic, when axe runs on the card and the record, then there is no violation
tests:
- Integration: the contract's role scope and tenant isolation.
- Playwright J2 to J3 handoff (fullstack): doctor sets 2 weeks → receptionist sees and books it.
notes:
- Regression risk: low.

=== MDV-P1-103
title: One queue rule for the clinic: the doctor's day follows it, shows tokens and puts nurse escalations first
type: Story
epic: MDV-P1-E12
prd: P2-01, P1-08
screens: D-01, P-05
priority: MUST
roles: Doctor, Nurse, Receptionist
labels: today, queue, front-desk, safety, tv, epic: ux programme, track: safety, wave: S2
estimate: 3
weeks: 6-7
confidence: 68 | The ordering is a back-end rule plus a setting; the clinic's rule is not known yet.
sources: docs/product-ux-audit/10-prioritized-implementation-backlog.md (audit backlog UX-10); docs/product-ux-audit/04-evidence-backed-findings.md (F-07, F-15); docs/product-ux-audit/reviews/reviewer-a-clinical-workflow.md (A-08, A-12); docs/product-ux-audit/tools/walkthrough-handoff.mjs
desc:
Programme: MDV-P1-E12 · UX programme: safe, cohesive clinic workspace (from the Nov 2026 product UX audit). Track S (safety, trust and handoff behaviour), wave S2. This is audit backlog UX-10 of the Nov 2026 product UX audit (an audit id, not an MDV key).

Persona and route: /today (doctor), the queue display, the board.
Findings and reviewers: F-07, F-15 (order) · reviewers A-08, A-12.
Rationale: A 72-year-old escalated for fever and BP 168/96 was row 11 of 11 on the doctor's list, below the first screen, because rows sort by slot time and a walk-in holds a late nominal slot. The display listed A-05, B-02, A-06, A-09, A-07, A-08: patients holding A-07 watch A-09 go first.
Proposed UX behaviour: A clinic setting chooses the queue rule: token order, arrival order or appointment order (and whether booked patients go before walk-ins). Arrived patients sort by that rule on the doctor's day, on the board within a column and on the queue display, so the three always agree. Every row on the doctor's day shows the token. A nurse escalation is pinned above Next patient with its reason, in words. A walk-in is ordered by arrival, not by a far slot time. The display still shows tokens only, and names the doctor per token after a reassignment.
Prototype and blueprint reference: docs/product-ux-audit/concepts/medvya-workspace-concept.html, doctor view, queue rail (audit note 3).
Components and tokens affected: Today list rows; Chip for Escalated. Setting in Settings → Clinical. No new component.
Front end and back end split: Back end: the ordering rule, the setting (audited), one ordered-queue query used by all three screens. Front end: Today, display, board.
Prerequisites and blocked by: None in code. The pilot clinic's queue rule must be known.
Clinical sign-off: Decision required before merge: question 10 of the validation plan (pilot doctor and receptionist), recorded on the issue.
Effort and regression risk: M · Medium: three screens and the live updates share the order.
Deliberately NOT in this ticket: (1) The queue rail inside the consultation (its own ticket). (2) Token slip printing (its own ticket). (3) Wait-time estimates.
ac:
- Given a nurse escalation, when the doctor opens the day at 1024 by 768, then the escalated visit is in the first viewport above Next patient with its reason in words
- Given the clinic's rule, when the display's Next list, the board column and the doctor's day are compared, then the order is the same
- Given arrived patients, when the doctor's day renders, then each row shows its token
- Given a walk-in who arrived at 09:58 with a nominal 19:00 slot, when the arrival rule is set, then she sorts by 09:58
- Given the ordered-queue query, when the escalation-first rule is removed, then the integration test fails; a change of the setting is audited and scoped to the tenant
- Given the queue display, when it is read, then it shows tokens and the doctor per token and no name
- Given English and Arabic, when axe runs on the doctor's day, then there is no violation and escalation is not conveyed by colour alone
tests:
- Integration: ordering for the three rules, escalation, tenant isolation.
- Playwright, real stack: run the handoff walkthrough steps (J3 steps 4 to 6) → the escalated patient is row 1 on the doctor's day.
- tests/e2e/today.spec.ts, queue-tv.spec.ts.
notes:
- Regression risk: medium.

=== MDV-P1-104
title: The nurse's chief complaint prefills the doctor's note, marked from nurse intake
type: Story
epic: MDV-P1-E12
prd: P2-03, P2-04
screens: N-01, E-04
priority: SHOULD
roles: Nurse, Doctor
labels: nurse, notes, encounters, provenance, epic: ux programme, track: safety, wave: S2, needs: clinical sign-off
estimate: 1
weeks: 5
confidence: 72 | Whether the nurse's text is stored was not tested; if not, a small back-end change.
sources: docs/product-ux-audit/10-prioritized-implementation-backlog.md (audit backlog UX-11); docs/product-ux-audit/04-evidence-backed-findings.md (F-16); docs/product-ux-audit/reviews/reviewer-a-clinical-workflow.md (A-09); docs/product-ux-audit/08-design-system-blueprint.md (section 9, Redundant Entry)
desc:
Programme: MDV-P1-E12 · UX programme: safe, cohesive clinic workspace (from the Nov 2026 product UX audit). Track S (safety, trust and handoff behaviour), wave S2. This is audit backlog UX-11 of the Nov 2026 product UX audit (an audit id, not an MDV key).

Persona and route: /nurse/:id → /consult/:id · nurse, doctor.
Findings and reviewers: F-16 · reviewer A-09. WCAG 2.2 SC 3.3.7 Redundant Entry, across two users.
Rationale: The nurse typed Fever and sore throat for three days and saved. In the consultation, Chief complaint was empty and the text appeared nowhere. The doctor asks and types again.
Proposed UX behaviour: When the consultation opens and the doctor's Chief complaint is empty, it is prefilled with the nurse's text and marked from nurse intake with the nurse's name and time (the existing provenance tag). It is editable; once the doctor edits it, the text is the doctor's and the mark says edited by the doctor. A complaint the doctor already typed is never overwritten. The signed note records the source.
Prototype and blueprint reference: docs/product-ux-audit/08-design-system-blueprint.md section 9.
Components and tokens affected: NoteEditor chief complaint field; ProvenanceTag. No new component.
Front end and back end split: Front end; back end if the nurse's complaint is not yet stored or not exposed to the note.
Prerequisites and blocked by: None.
Clinical sign-off: Required before merge: question 8 of the validation plan (pilot doctor and nurse), recorded on the issue.
Effort and regression risk: S · Low. Unknowns stay blank: no complaint from the nurse means an empty field.
Deliberately NOT in this ticket: (1) Prefilling any other note section. (2) AI drafting.
ac:
- Given the nurse saved a complaint at intake, when the doctor opens the consultation, then Chief complaint holds that text with the mark from nurse intake
- Given the doctor already typed a complaint, when intake is saved later, then the doctor's text stays
- Given the prefilled text, when the doctor changes it, then the mark shows the doctor edited it and the signed note records both sources
- Given no complaint at intake, when the consultation opens, then the field is empty and nothing is guessed
- Given a screen reader, when the field is focused, then the source is read; axe finds no violation in English or Arabic
tests:
- Playwright J3 step 5 to J2 (fullstack): nurse types a complaint → the doctor's field holds it with the source mark.
- tests/e2e/nurse-intake.spec.ts, consult-note.spec.ts.
notes:
- Regression risk: low.

=== MDV-P1-105
title: Redeploy Azure dev from main, capture it again at 768 and 375 px, and mark its synthetic patients
